{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-114003",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+1)=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u={cos}^{3}⁡x+1.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={cos}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $9/8$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{9/8}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{9/8}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{2}^{9/8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{8})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{8})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{(\\frac{9}{8})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{16}{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
